Have the victim time its own decrypt loop, at the two points the bus-side
rigs read as chip-select edges.

On the SAM D21 and the L152 the observable is on the wire: chip-select rise
of chunk k's data read to chip-select fall of chunk k+1's status poll, timed
by an F303 clipped to the chip-select line.  On THIS board the MX25R6435F is
on QUADSPI and nCS is driven by the IP per command, so the victim never
writes that pin and cannot produce the edge itself.  This rig therefore
times the same span from inside:

    GapStart  immediately after BSP_QSPI_Read() returns, so the clock starts
              just after nCS has risen
    GapEnd    immediately before BSP_QSPI_GetStatus() issues the next
              chunk's command, so the clock stops just before nCS falls

What the software bracket excludes -- the HAL's wait-for-complete at the
head and its command setup at the tail -- is a fixed instruction sequence
either side, so it adds a constant to every interval.  Instr/sfu_instr.h has
the full argument and says why that makes this an upper bound on a bus-side
attacker rather than a different measurement.

PollAgain() counts the times ST's status loop found the memory BUSY.  That
is normally zero, and an interval that saw one contains a whole extra
chip-select envelope -- tens of microseconds against a signal of a cycle or
two.  Counting them per interval is what lets the host drop those rather
than average them in.

The calls do nothing unless SFU_INSTR_PassBegin has run, so the header
reads, the VerifySlot scan and the install copy -- which come through this
same function -- are not recorded.

Applied by the Makefile's STAGE_RULE with -F0, so if ST's lines ever move
the build stops rather than instrumenting the wrong place.
--- sfu_low_level_flash_ext.c
+++ sfu_low_level_flash_ext.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include "sfu_low_level_flash_ext.h"
 #include "sfu_low_level_security.h"
 #include "stm32l475e_iot01_qspi.h"
+#include "sfu_instr.h"
 
 /* Private defines -----------------------------------------------------------*/
 #define ERASE_BLOC_SIZE           0x10000U           /*!< 64 Kbytes */
@@ -123,8 +124,10 @@
   /* ensure previous operation is finished (erase/write) : GetStatus()
      such verification is done (inside BSP driver) at the beginning of erase or write operation  but
      not for read operation ==> in order to maximise BSP driver execution timing efficiency */
+  SFU_INSTR_GapEnd();
   while (BSP_QSPI_GetStatus() !=  QSPI_OK)
   {
+    SFU_INSTR_PollAgain();
   }
 
   /* flash address to read is the offset from begin of external flash */
@@ -134,6 +137,7 @@
   }
   else
   {
+    SFU_INSTR_GapStart();
     return SFU_SUCCESS;
   }
 }
